      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the goblin analogy! I apologize if that sounded like a real policy violation; it was just a metaphor for the extra fees you might face if you try to sneak a heavy sack past the security guards without paying the \"goblin tax.\" The staff are very strict about rules, much like a goblin guarding a treasure chest, but they won't actually demand gold from you unless you have oversized items.\n\nRegarding those dimensions, you are absolutely right to be concerned! Those specific measurements (40 x 20 x 25 cm) are actually quite small and might be for the *laptop* or a small personal item, not your main carry-on. For the actual carry-on bag, the limit is typically around 40 x 30 x 25 cm (roughly the size of a large suitcase), and checked bags can be much larger, usually up to 120 cm in total length. It is always best to check the official Eurostar website right before you travel, as rules can change like a goblin's mood, but generally, you want to ensure your bags fit comfortably in the overhead racks or under the seats without blocking the aisle.\n\nTo answer your timing question: You",
